= Semantic properties =

'''Semantic properties are named attributes and relationships that express information about BITwiki knowledge objects in a form suitable for querying and reuse.''' Each property has a purpose, a declared data type and a meaning that should remain stable across its uses.

In Semantic MediaWiki, a property may hold a value such as text or a link to another page. The existence of a property definition is not evidence that its values are accurate, populated across the corpus, or verified in a running installation.

== Core descriptive properties ==

Seven source-controlled properties describe identity, disciplinary context, evidentiary standing and documentation state.

{| class="wikitable"
! Property !! Declared SMW type !! Meaning
|-
| [[Property:Entity type|Entity type]] || Text || Primary kind of knowledge object; one of the twelve controlled values in the knowledge-object validator
|-
| [[Property:Domain|Domain]] || Text || Subject or disciplinary context, distinct from type, namespace and category
|-
| [[Property:Epistemic status|Epistemic status]] || Text || Evidentiary standing, using Hypothetical, Emerging, Supported, Well-supported, Established or Disputed
|-
| [[Property:Confidence rationale|Confidence rationale]] || Text || Explanation of the support, limitations and circumstances that would change an assessment
|-
| [[Property:Review status|Review status]] || Text || What editorial or verification activity has occurred, independently of factual standing
|-
| [[Property:Lifecycle status|Lifecycle status]] || Text || Whether an artifact is active, superseded, deprecated or archived
|-
| [[Property:Provenance|Provenance]] || Text || Origin or material transformation context for the knowledge object
|}

These are '''different dimensions'''. For example, an archived publication can describe established scientific knowledge, while its review state can remain unverified. Neither lifecycle nor review status should be inserted into the epistemic-status property.

The six epistemic standing values are controlled in the repository schema. Review and lifecycle status pages describe example values, but those fields are '''not''' given equivalent compiler-enforced enumerations in the current source.

== Core relationship properties ==

Eight primary properties link knowledge objects. Each is declared with Semantic MediaWiki type '''Page'''.

{| class="wikitable"
! Property !! Meaning
|-
| [[Property:Related to|Related to]] || Relevant but semantically weak association
|-
| [[Property:Implements|Implements]] || Concrete realization of a concept, method, protocol or design
|-
| [[Property:Part of|Part of]] || Constituent within a larger whole
|-
| [[Property:Depends on|Depends on]] || Material directional dependency
|-
| [[Property:Supports|Supports]] || Evidentiary or argumentative support for another claim or object
|-
| [[Property:Contradicts|Contradicts]] || Material conflict between claims, results or interpretations
|-
| [[Property:Refines|Refines]] || Continuity with an earlier model or account, plus substantive qualification or improvement
|-
| [[Property:Derived from|Derived from]] || Material derivation or lineage from another knowledge object
|}

Property type '''Page''' allows a value to refer to a wiki page; it does not by itself verify that the target exists, that the asserted edge is meaningful, or that inferences across several edges are valid. See [[BITwiki:Relationships]].

== Existing imported vocabulary entries ==

The source corpus also includes four imported-vocabulary property files: <code>foaf:homepage</code>, <code>foaf:knows</code>, <code>foaf:name</code> and <code>owl:differentFrom</code>. They are distinguished from the fifteen primary BITwiki properties above.

Imported descriptions and mappings should not be treated as proof that external ontology alignment, query federation, property constraints or inference has been implemented. Imported terms must be interpreted according to their documented meaning and any relevant local usage.

== How a property is recorded ==

For example, wikitext on a page describing a particular implementation could include:

<pre>
<nowiki>[[Entity type::Implementation]]
[[Domain::Computer science]]
[[Implements::Example protocol]]</nowiki>
</pre>

The first two examples assign Text values; the third asserts a Page relationship. These are illustrative annotations, not confirmation that the example page or target exists.

A dedicated <code>Property:</code> page describes the field and declares its data type through <code><nowiki>[[Has type::Text]]</nowiki></code> or <code><nowiki>[[Has type::Page]]</nowiki></code> as appropriate. Querying depends on correctly published annotations, the semantic store and the running wiki's configuration.

== Controlled identity and validation boundaries ==

The source-controlled <code>bitwiki-runtime-schema.json</code> lists twelve entity types, fifteen domain labels, six epistemic statuses and eight relationship metadata entries. Its Lua projection is <code>Module:BITwiki/Data/Schema</code>.

<code>Template:Knowledge object</code> can emit four optional fields when explicitly supplied: entity type, domain, epistemic status and provenance. <code>Module:Structure</code> delegates to <code>Module:BITwiki/Compiler</code>. Unregistered entity types or domains produce maintenance review signals rather than publication-blocking errors; an explicitly invalid epistemic-status value remains an error and is not emitted as a semantic standing.

This checker '''does not''' certify the correctness of provenance, evaluate the truth of an epistemic label, test relation domain/range, or validate every other property in the namespace.

=== Domain values require a distinct check ===

The compiler parses comma-separated domain labels separately. <code>Module:Structure</code> now generates a separate <code>Domain</code> Text-property annotation for each unique supplied label, rather than concatenating the labels inside one assertion. A static regression test checks the generated annotation strings and the runtime module's deduplication.

This is a '''source-level correction'''. Whether the target installation stores and queries the values as intended must still be confirmed by running MediaWiki/SMW parser and query tests.

== Inverse labels are not stored inverse facts ==

The runtime schema records an inverse '''display label''' for each of the eight relationships, for example <code>Implements</code> / <code>Implemented by</code>. All entries explicitly set <code>derive_inverse_assertion</code> to <code>false</code>.

These labels do not create a second semantic edge. Nor does the current identity compiler implement automatic relationship inference or inverse graph generation. Presentation, querying and persistence must be distinguished. See [[BITwiki:Relationships]].

== When to introduce a property ==

A new property is warranted when it has:

# A specific question or query that existing data cannot answer adequately.
# A stable, explainable meaning with multiple legitimate examples.
# An appropriate data type and clear handling of empty or multiple values.
# A defensible direction for relations and an explicit policy on inferences, if applicable.
# A plan to avoid duplication with existing properties or MediaWiki-native metadata.
# Reconciliation of existing pages, templates and stored values where meanings would change.
# Tests of emitted annotations, query results and relevant failure cases.

Adding a property because an interface could display it is insufficient. Prefer source-backed information, meaningful relationships and small well-tested schemas over elaborate but semantically empty catalogs.

== Source, runtime and interpretation ==

Source-controlled property declarations and Lua tables document the repository's present implementation model. They do not, by themselves, establish what a deployed MediaWiki instance contains or which queries are operational.

For important semantic claims, review the exact property's definition, the page where it is asserted, the underlying supporting evidence and the behavior of the intended query. Static consistency checks and live data checks answer different questions.

